<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jonas Basanavičius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1851–1927</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jonas Basanavičius was a Lithuanian physician, folklorist and leader of the national revival, often called the patriarch of the nation. He co-founded Aušra, the first Lithuanian-language newspaper, in 1883 while working as a doctor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. He chaired the Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seimas">Seimas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> in 1905 and presided over the session of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-lithuania">Council of Lithuania</a> that signed the Act of Independence on 16 February 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jonas-basanavicius.jpg" alt="Jonas Basanavičius" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aleksandras Jurašaitis (1859-1915) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jonas_Basanavicius_(1851-1927).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
